1
00:00:00,001 --> 00:00:07,360
1920s and 30s, in the big cities and small towns, they earned a fearsome reputation in a blaze of bullets.

2
00:00:08,320 --> 00:00:13,280
For the criminal and lawmen alike, they were a best friend or a deadly enemy.

3
00:00:14,320 --> 00:00:18,640
A pull of a trigger could end a life of violence or give birth to infamy.

4
00:00:19,680 --> 00:00:23,280
Now, Gangster Guns on Modern Marvels.

5
00:00:30,000 --> 00:00:40,340
John Dillinger

6
00:00:40,341 --> 00:00:44,540
Pretty Boy Floyd

7
00:00:44,541 --> 00:00:48,700
Babyface Nelson

8
00:00:48,701 --> 00:00:52,760
Al Capone

9
00:00:52,761 --> 00:00:58,000
Bonnie and Clyde

10
00:01:00,000 --> 00:01:05,000
The names evoke images of outlaw whiskey, bank holdups,

11
00:01:05,001 --> 00:01:06,000
and bloody shootouts.

12
00:01:10,000 --> 00:01:13,000
The guns they used were just as notorious.

13
00:01:13,001 --> 00:01:21,000
Colt .45s and .38s, the Tommy gun, Whippet guns,

14
00:01:24,000 --> 00:01:29,000
the Browning automatic rifle, and the R.C.

15
00:01:30,000 --> 00:01:41,740
These legendary weapons are forever linked with the storied lives of the gangsters of

16
00:01:41,741 --> 00:01:43,440
the 1920s and 30s.

17
00:01:45,300 --> 00:01:50,240
Their exploits filled the headlines, but it was their firepower that brought them fame.

18
00:01:52,980 --> 00:01:56,800
Gangsters employed pocket guns, which could be easily concealed.

19
00:02:00,000 --> 00:02:02,140
It's not a terribly effective caliber.

20
00:02:02,460 --> 00:02:04,460
32 is more effective than 25 was.

21
00:02:04,520 --> 00:02:07,560
25 is the most common pocket gun or pocket caliber.

22
00:02:08,060 --> 00:02:10,060
But it beats throwing rocks.

23
00:02:12,220 --> 00:02:16,380
Gangsters were also the first to use automatic weapons outside of the military.

24
00:02:19,080 --> 00:02:22,560
Some of these guys came out firing, and this is what they came out firing.

25
00:02:23,100 --> 00:02:25,160
And it was scary to go against something like this.

26
00:02:25,161 --> 00:02:36,400
The desire to win at all costs set the gangsters of this era apart from any other criminal element in American history.

27
00:02:38,400 --> 00:02:49,960
They seem to be incredibly bold and daring and have great presence of mind in the face of fire with bullets whizzing by their head.

28
00:02:49,961 --> 00:02:52,740
They seemed entirely unaffected by that.

29
00:02:53,400 --> 00:02:59,600
So some of these jobs they pulled might have been extraordinarily difficult,

30
00:03:00,040 --> 00:03:02,220
but they were there for the duration.

31
00:03:06,120 --> 00:03:11,200
But how and why did gangsters become so well-armed in the early part of the 20th century?

32
00:03:11,720 --> 00:03:15,100
The answer is simple, alcohol and greed.

33
00:03:15,101 --> 00:03:22,980
When the United States passed the Volstead Act in 1919,

34
00:03:23,440 --> 00:03:24,580
enacting prohibition,

35
00:03:24,940 --> 00:03:27,280
it was like an open invitation for criminals.

36
00:03:34,220 --> 00:03:36,140
Well, people continued to drink.

37
00:03:36,820 --> 00:03:38,440
Overall in the United States,

38
00:03:38,540 --> 00:03:41,600
drinking may have gone down very marginally,

39
00:03:41,601 --> 00:03:45,560
but this meant now bootleggers were supplying that liquor.

40
00:03:46,240 --> 00:03:50,040
People made enormous money in now the illicit traffic.

41
00:03:50,620 --> 00:03:53,660
So really prohibition spawned this era.

42
00:03:54,240 --> 00:03:57,380
At the same time in small towns across middle America,

43
00:03:57,740 --> 00:03:59,660
another brand of criminal was flourishing,

44
00:04:00,140 --> 00:04:01,420
the motorized bandit.

45
00:04:05,440 --> 00:04:09,260
Bonnie and Clyde, Pretty Boy Floyd, and Babyface Nelson

46
00:04:09,261 --> 00:04:11,300
derived their income not from booze,

47
00:04:11,600 --> 00:04:12,940
but from robbing banks.

48
00:04:15,840 --> 00:04:18,800
Though not as sophisticated as their big city counterparts,

49
00:04:19,240 --> 00:04:21,780
the motorized bandits became just as well-known

50
00:04:21,781 --> 00:04:23,300
and just as deadly.

51
00:04:25,460 --> 00:04:29,320
If one understands that Bonnie Parker was 4 feet 10 inches tall

52
00:04:29,321 --> 00:04:32,760
and weighed 85 pounds, and she could run and hip-shoot

53
00:04:32,761 --> 00:04:35,020
a 20-pound machine rifle like a Marine,

54
00:04:35,400 --> 00:04:37,500
then you get a thoroughly different picture

55
00:04:37,501 --> 00:04:41,040
of who Bonnie Parker was and of what the Barrel Gang was about.

56
00:04:41,600 --> 00:04:49,740
Today, the image of the 20s and 30s is often one of incredible violence and lawlessness.

57
00:04:50,440 --> 00:04:55,260
But in many respects, the era was one of the most crime-free periods in American history.

58
00:04:57,160 --> 00:05:02,340
There wasn't much crime against innocent citizens during the 1920s and 1930s.

59
00:05:02,640 --> 00:05:08,820
When the gangsters and outlaws killed people, they were from rival gangs or they were lawmen.

60
00:05:08,821 --> 00:05:22,640
In the late 1920s, my mother told me that with my father, they would walk through Central Park to after-hours clubs up in Harlem with never a thought of danger.

61
00:05:23,340 --> 00:05:26,886
There was no such thing as mugging. There was no such thing as rape.

62
00:05:26,887 --> 00:05:28,920
That would have outraged the community.

63
00:05:31,660 --> 00:05:37,320
The criminals of the day adopted a strict code of conduct regarding the protection of innocent civilians.

64
00:05:38,820 --> 00:05:42,400
And as the Roaring Twenties gave way to the despair of the Great Depression,

65
00:05:43,000 --> 00:05:45,400
many gangsters became romanticized figures.

66
00:05:49,160 --> 00:05:52,960
In particular, the public viewed the motorized bandits of the Midwest

67
00:05:52,961 --> 00:05:55,100
as latter-day Robin Hoods.

68
00:05:56,400 --> 00:06:00,020
To properly understand the gangster era of the 1920s and 30s,

69
00:06:00,040 --> 00:06:01,840
you first must know how America was.

70
00:06:02,500 --> 00:06:06,100
In that time, you had poverty beyond the ability of most people today to imagine.

71
00:06:06,740 --> 00:06:08,220
Racism was the law of the land,

72
00:06:08,440 --> 00:06:10,620
and firearms of any kind were available to anyone.

73
00:06:12,000 --> 00:06:14,400
There were a series of high-profile criminals

74
00:06:14,401 --> 00:06:16,320
who attracted the public's attention.

75
00:06:17,280 --> 00:06:20,620
Law enforcement in those days could only go to,

76
00:06:20,621 --> 00:06:23,160
a lot of cases, city limits, county limits,

77
00:06:23,360 --> 00:06:24,760
and to state limits that stop.

78
00:06:25,040 --> 00:06:28,900
Well, then the guys could flee in peace

79
00:06:28,901 --> 00:06:30,660
to the next state or the next county.

80
00:06:32,680 --> 00:06:35,580
Both big city gangsters and the motorized bandits

81
00:06:35,581 --> 00:06:38,400
borrowed their tactics from another romanticized age

82
00:06:38,401 --> 00:06:41,140
of American lawlessness, the Old West.

83
00:06:45,180 --> 00:06:48,400
Many of the criminals who got their start in the 1920s

84
00:06:48,401 --> 00:06:50,840
had grown up hearing stories about outlaws

85
00:06:50,841 --> 00:06:53,360
like Billy the Kid and Jesse James.

86
00:06:56,680 --> 00:06:59,540
Rumor has it that Clyde Barrow studied the tactics

87
00:06:59,541 --> 00:07:01,100
employed during the Civil War

88
00:07:01,101 --> 00:07:03,720
by infamous Confederate raider Bill Anderson.

89
00:07:05,580 --> 00:07:09,080
Barrow favored the same run-and-gun techniques

90
00:07:09,081 --> 00:07:11,580
that made Anderson's unit one of the most feared

91
00:07:11,581 --> 00:07:13,580
by Union soldiers.

92
00:07:17,580 --> 00:07:20,580
Prohibition and money gave the gangsters and bandits

93
00:07:20,581 --> 00:07:23,580
of the 1920s opportunity and motive.

94
00:07:27,580 --> 00:07:30,580
But it was World War I that gave them firepower.

95
00:07:35,580 --> 00:07:42,380
When the war ended, the United States was flooded with millions of weapons that returned

96
00:07:42,381 --> 00:07:46,820
home with soldiers from the front.

97
00:07:46,821 --> 00:07:51,760
Because the nation had almost no gun laws, it was possible for a private citizen to own

98
00:07:51,761 --> 00:08:04,340
even the most powerful military machine guns.

99
00:08:04,341 --> 00:08:09,220
Despite the efforts of gun manufacturers to keep their weapons out of the hands of criminals,

100
00:08:09,221 --> 00:08:13,500
it was easy for almost anyone to get a hold of serious firepower.

101
00:08:13,501 --> 00:08:18,500
Actually, you could buy them on the commercial market.

102
00:08:18,501 --> 00:08:23,580
Colt did make a commercial model, the R-75, and the Colt monitor was available if somebody

103
00:08:23,581 --> 00:08:25,200
had enough money.

104
00:08:25,201 --> 00:08:29,460
Colt tried to regulate who the guns went to, but it was quite easy to have a front man.

105
00:08:29,461 --> 00:08:36,280
There were several gunsmiths in Chicago, in the Illinois areas, who would purchase weapons

106
00:08:36,281 --> 00:08:41,660
for the underworld.

107
00:08:41,661 --> 00:08:46,460
The access to high powered weapons, fueled by the potential to make millions of dollars,

108
00:08:46,461 --> 00:08:53,460
gave the outlaws of the era a unique and deadly characteristic, an aura of invincibility.

109
00:08:53,461 --> 00:09:02,840
But before big gangster guns blazed across America, gangsters favored a small, easily

110
00:09:02,841 --> 00:09:05,680
concealed weapon to do their bidding.

111
00:09:05,681 --> 00:09:08,920
Their choice was the pocket gun.

112
00:09:08,921 --> 00:09:15,620
Before 1934, FBI agents were not allowed to carry guns or make arrests.

113
00:09:15,621 --> 00:09:20,700
They also needed the help of local law enforcement to take suspects into custody.

114
00:09:20,701 --> 00:09:27,180
guns will continue on modern marvels

115
00:09:28,060 --> 00:09:31,900
even though the gangster era was ultimately defined by big powerful

116
00:09:31,901 --> 00:09:35,580
automatic weapons outlaws initially believed smaller was

117
00:09:35,581 --> 00:09:37,980
better

118
00:09:38,380 --> 00:09:43,580
pocket guns were compact and easy to conceal they were also highly popular

119
00:09:43,581 --> 00:09:46,940
on both sides of the law

120
00:09:46,941 --> 00:09:53,620
For many people, the pocket gun was the great equalizer that helped keep society in check.

121
00:09:55,620 --> 00:10:01,000
Pocket guns to me bring back a more civilized era.

122
00:10:03,560 --> 00:10:07,580
I very much believe in politeness and being courteous to one another.

123
00:10:08,220 --> 00:10:10,200
And you should do it of your own volition.

124
00:10:10,201 --> 00:10:15,840
But in the 20s and 30s, and it didn't matter if you were in Chicago or if you were in South Georgia

125
00:10:15,841 --> 00:10:18,880
or if you were somewhere in the Midwest or Arizona,

126
00:10:19,720 --> 00:10:21,860
you were polite to someone if for no other reason

127
00:10:21,861 --> 00:10:23,860
you didn't know what they had their hand on in their pocket.

128
00:10:24,500 --> 00:10:27,040
And so it would behoove you to be on your best behavior,

129
00:10:27,260 --> 00:10:29,860
not insult a man's lady, don't kick his dog,

130
00:10:29,940 --> 00:10:30,800
leave his children alone,

131
00:10:30,840 --> 00:10:33,060
because he just might reach in his pocket and put a bullet in you.

132
00:10:36,060 --> 00:10:38,440
Pocket guns didn't pack much of a wallop,

133
00:10:38,740 --> 00:10:40,940
but in the early days of organized crime,

134
00:10:40,941 --> 00:10:43,460
getting the drop on someone was more important

135
00:10:43,461 --> 00:10:44,980
than the size of your gun.

136
00:10:45,840 --> 00:10:52,880
The most favored pocket guns were either a .25 or .32 caliber,

137
00:10:53,460 --> 00:10:55,180
usually manufactured by Colt.

138
00:10:57,620 --> 00:11:01,280
The guns were easily concealed inside a pair of pleated pants

139
00:11:01,281 --> 00:11:02,900
or in a shoulder holster.

140
00:11:04,440 --> 00:11:08,260
Well, right now I'm loading the magazine for a Colt 1903 pocket pistol

141
00:11:08,261 --> 00:11:09,440
in .32 caliber.

142
00:11:10,140 --> 00:11:12,700
And this was a very popular, very common caliber,

143
00:11:12,701 --> 00:11:18,800
a very common pistol from about 1903 up until just recently here.

144
00:11:19,900 --> 00:11:22,740
I'm placed in another magazine, the hollow of the gun.

145
00:11:23,640 --> 00:11:25,840
A gun just about like this was used by Bonnie Parker

146
00:11:25,841 --> 00:11:27,420
to break Clyde out of jail in Waco.

147
00:11:28,180 --> 00:11:31,240
The story goes that she had one of these taped to her thigh

148
00:11:31,241 --> 00:11:34,060
when she busted Clyde out of the Waco jail,

149
00:11:35,000 --> 00:11:36,680
and he went on another spree.

150
00:11:36,960 --> 00:11:38,680
Now, Bonnie Parker was a pretty small woman.

151
00:11:38,820 --> 00:11:40,460
They say she was about 4'11", I believe,

152
00:11:40,461 --> 00:11:44,120
and so I don't imagine she had much of a thigh so you can imagine that's

153
00:11:44,121 --> 00:11:47,680
something for the concealability of the gun

154
00:11:49,060 --> 00:11:56,040
the problem with larger pistols like a 45 was obvious even under a coat everyone

155
00:11:56,041 --> 00:12:02,100
knew when a gangster was packing but the downside with the pocket guns gave them

156
00:12:02,101 --> 00:12:09,060
limited life as criminals became more brazen in fact many gangsters of the

157
00:12:09,061 --> 00:12:16,060
1920s came to consider pocket guns as their last chance weapons.

158
00:12:16,061 --> 00:12:21,060
This is the magazine for a Colt 25 1908 pocket automatic.

159
00:12:21,061 --> 00:12:26,060
The whole idea behind a 25 automatic is it is the last resort.

160
00:12:26,061 --> 00:12:31,060
It's either this or a pocket knife before you're pretty much out of business.

161
00:12:31,061 --> 00:12:34,060
It holds six rounds.

162
00:12:34,061 --> 00:12:37,660
They were available in nickel plate or blue.

163
00:12:39,360 --> 00:12:40,880
Several variations on grips.

164
00:12:40,980 --> 00:12:45,760
You could get hard rubber, bakelite, wood, pearl by custom order.

165
00:12:46,340 --> 00:12:47,500
It was good in a vest pocket.

166
00:12:48,000 --> 00:12:50,500
A lot of people would carry these on the end of a watch fob,

167
00:12:50,720 --> 00:12:52,840
so you wouldn't know what the other end of the pocket watch was.

168
00:12:53,380 --> 00:12:55,040
These were very popular with everyone.

169
00:12:59,780 --> 00:13:03,060
But as gangs grew more violent, police fought back.

170
00:13:03,061 --> 00:13:07,200
and criminals quickly realized they needed to increase their firepower.

171
00:13:10,540 --> 00:13:13,580
Rifles and shotguns were at first seen as too cumbersome.

172
00:13:16,920 --> 00:13:21,080
Instead, gangsters of the day wanted a gun with a lot of stopping power

173
00:13:21,081 --> 00:13:23,520
that could still fit in the palm of their hand.

174
00:13:25,340 --> 00:13:30,620
The answer, two automatic pistols, the .45 and the Super .38.

175
00:13:33,060 --> 00:13:34,640
Both were large handguns.

176
00:13:34,920 --> 00:13:36,660
Both could kill a man where he stood.

177
00:13:44,480 --> 00:13:46,900
It's said that a gangster named Leslie Homer

178
00:13:46,901 --> 00:13:49,640
gave the Super 38 its greatest endorsement

179
00:13:49,641 --> 00:13:53,060
while being interrogated by police in Racine, Wisconsin.

180
00:13:54,820 --> 00:13:57,200
Racine Police Department had lost one of its members

181
00:13:57,201 --> 00:13:58,460
to the John Dillinger gang,

182
00:13:58,780 --> 00:14:01,640
and any time that they encountered a member of that gang,

183
00:14:01,641 --> 00:14:04,800
they gave him their undivided attention, as good cops will.

184
00:14:05,840 --> 00:14:08,180
And late in 1933, they sacked up Leslie Homer,

185
00:14:08,620 --> 00:14:10,140
who was a runner for the Dillinger gang,

186
00:14:10,260 --> 00:14:11,660
took him downtown and sweated him.

187
00:14:12,320 --> 00:14:15,160
And the words from Homer spilled out into newspapers

188
00:14:15,161 --> 00:14:16,920
and police stations all over the country.

189
00:14:17,180 --> 00:14:18,820
And one of the things he said was

190
00:14:18,821 --> 00:14:21,160
that if you want to give your coppers an even break,

191
00:14:21,400 --> 00:14:23,720
you need to get them one of those new Super 38 guns.

192
00:14:23,820 --> 00:14:27,460
A gun of that type will blow a hole in any bulletproof vest made.

193
00:14:31,640 --> 00:14:35,080
But even high-powered handguns required accuracy.

194
00:14:35,840 --> 00:14:39,700
In the run-and-gun style of the gangsters, there was little time to take aim.

195
00:14:44,540 --> 00:14:47,160
Pistols worked best in close-fire situations.

196
00:14:48,000 --> 00:14:52,440
For robbing banks or shooting at an enemy from a moving car, something better was needed.

197
00:14:56,740 --> 00:15:01,400
Clyde Barrow for one didn't want to wait for the firearms industry to solve the problem.

198
00:15:01,640 --> 00:15:02,840
He did it himself.

199
00:15:06,140 --> 00:15:09,040
Clyde was known to be an expert with shotguns.

200
00:15:09,620 --> 00:15:12,580
He believed if he could combine the power of a shotgun

201
00:15:12,581 --> 00:15:18,100
with a smaller weapon that was easier to wield,

202
00:15:18,780 --> 00:15:20,340
he'd have the perfect gun.

203
00:15:24,600 --> 00:15:27,360
He found the solution by studying history.

204
00:15:28,160 --> 00:15:31,860
A century earlier, a Mormon by the name of Oren Porter Rockwell

205
00:15:31,861 --> 00:15:34,740
had developed a gun to protect his brethren in Utah.

206
00:15:37,740 --> 00:15:40,960
Rockwell cut the end of the stock in the barrel off a shotgun

207
00:15:40,961 --> 00:15:43,320
to give the weapon greater scatter effect.

208
00:15:45,520 --> 00:15:48,140
Clyde was convinced the same technique would work

209
00:15:48,141 --> 00:15:51,360
with the more powerful shotguns of the 1920s and 30s.

210
00:15:53,000 --> 00:15:56,420
He started sawing the ends off of Remington shotguns.

211
00:15:56,421 --> 00:15:59,160
He called his creation a whippet gun,

212
00:15:59,760 --> 00:16:02,560
supposedly because he could whip it out in an instant.

213
00:16:06,500 --> 00:16:09,040
This gun is a replica of a weapon

214
00:16:09,041 --> 00:16:12,100
that Clyde Barrow and Bonnie Parker used back in the 1930s.

215
00:16:12,800 --> 00:16:15,380
This gun was modified by having the barrel cut off

216
00:16:15,381 --> 00:16:18,220
to about a shotgun shell's length

217
00:16:18,221 --> 00:16:19,700
past the end of the magazine tube

218
00:16:19,701 --> 00:16:21,060
where the extra shells were held.

219
00:16:22,140 --> 00:16:24,240
If you'll notice of the design of the strap,

220
00:16:24,880 --> 00:16:26,060
it loops over the shoulder,

221
00:16:26,420 --> 00:16:32,320
But what happens is, if you hung it, you'd bring it up and it would swing it up to the shoulder.

222
00:16:33,100 --> 00:16:39,100
It wasn't meant to be used like you see in the movies, like this, like you see in all the action pictures nowadays.

223
00:16:39,680 --> 00:16:45,420
These were designed primarily to be shoulder fire weapons by people who were actually using it who were of very small stature.

224
00:16:48,880 --> 00:16:55,100
As the gangster era progressed, one thing that set the outlaws apart was how good they were with their guns.

225
00:16:56,420 --> 00:17:05,120
Not only did they know their weapons and experiment with significant modifications to them,

226
00:17:05,420 --> 00:17:07,920
but they were some of the best marksmen of the age.

227
00:17:09,940 --> 00:17:14,320
They went out in the woods, they went into rural areas, they practiced, they knew their weapons,

228
00:17:14,440 --> 00:17:18,780
they cleaned their weapons, they broke them down because they knew you lived and died with your

229
00:17:18,781 --> 00:17:23,169
weapons. So they just didn't spray bullets all over the area.

230
00:17:23,170 --> 00:17:25,760
Many of these gunmen, the enforcers,

231
00:17:25,761 --> 00:17:27,820
for these gangs were deadly marksmen,

232
00:17:27,920 --> 00:17:29,460
and they practiced regularly.

233
00:17:31,780 --> 00:17:34,600
But as the gangs acquired bigger and better guns,

234
00:17:34,860 --> 00:17:36,300
so did law enforcement.

235
00:17:40,820 --> 00:17:43,760
By the mid-1920s, it became obvious

236
00:17:43,761 --> 00:17:45,300
to the more ruthless gangsters

237
00:17:45,301 --> 00:17:47,520
that in order to stay one step ahead

238
00:17:47,521 --> 00:17:49,400
of both the police and the competition,

239
00:17:49,960 --> 00:17:51,780
drastic steps needed to be taken.

240
00:17:52,900 --> 00:17:55,460
Being a good shot was no longer enough.

241
00:17:55,760 --> 00:18:00,760
messages of intimidation would now be delivered in a hail of lead.

242
00:18:00,761 --> 00:18:05,760
The machine gun was about to become the gangster's weapon of choice.

243
00:18:11,760 --> 00:18:15,760
While awaiting trial for the murder of an East Chicago police officer,

244
00:18:15,761 --> 00:18:20,760
John Dillinger escaped from the Indiana Crown Point County Jail using a wooden gun

245
00:18:20,761 --> 00:18:23,760
he later claimed he made out of a washboard.

246
00:18:23,761 --> 00:18:28,760
Gangster Guns will continue on Modern Marvels.

247
00:18:32,760 --> 00:18:36,760
If there was one gun that let everyone know a criminal had arrived,

248
00:18:36,761 --> 00:18:38,760
it was the Tommy Gun.

249
00:18:41,760 --> 00:18:44,760
Made famous by the gangster movies of Hollywood,

250
00:18:44,761 --> 00:18:47,760
the Tommy Gun was the perfect criminal weapon.

251
00:18:47,761 --> 00:18:52,760
It was small, it had style, and it threw a lot of lead.

252
00:18:52,761 --> 00:18:58,020
This is a Colt Thompson 1921A.

253
00:18:58,900 --> 00:19:03,040
It has a fire rate of about 900 rounds a minute.

254
00:19:04,120 --> 00:19:08,500
It was very popular in the 20s and 30s with both sides of the law

255
00:19:08,501 --> 00:19:14,780
due to the fact that it shot the .45 Colt cartridge,

256
00:19:15,200 --> 00:19:18,040
which is well known for its man-stopping ability,

257
00:19:18,800 --> 00:19:21,820
and it delivered a lot of lead in a hurry.

258
00:19:22,760 --> 00:19:29,960
The Tommy gun has been forever linked to Al Capone and his bootlegging activities in Chicago.

259
00:19:33,040 --> 00:19:36,500
Ironically, Capone himself only fired a Tommy gun once.

260
00:19:36,920 --> 00:19:38,840
He had his henchmen do the dirty work.

261
00:19:42,080 --> 00:19:47,200
The most notorious use of the weapon was the 1929 St. Valentine's Day Massacre,

262
00:19:47,480 --> 00:19:49,900
where Capone went after a rival gang.

263
00:19:49,901 --> 00:19:58,240
Out the window of a car or in close quarters, these are literally a bullet hose.

264
00:19:58,740 --> 00:20:02,140
You can just take this gun and just hose the room down in a matter of seconds.

265
00:20:02,940 --> 00:20:05,580
And very little that gets hit with it gets back up.

266
00:20:11,500 --> 00:20:15,200
Though the public immediately associated the Tommy gun with outlaws,

267
00:20:15,600 --> 00:20:18,220
many of the most famous gangsters never used it.

268
00:20:19,900 --> 00:20:23,900
John Dillinger had his picture taken holding a Thompson,

269
00:20:23,901 --> 00:20:25,900
but he was a very bad shot.

270
00:20:28,900 --> 00:20:31,900
Bonnie and Clyde, perhaps the most famous gangsters associated

271
00:20:31,901 --> 00:20:34,900
with big guns, never owned a Tommy gun.

272
00:20:36,900 --> 00:20:39,900
For the Barrel Gang, the Tommy gun wasn't powerful enough.

273
00:20:41,900 --> 00:20:44,900
Though the Thompson could spit out .45 caliber slugs

274
00:20:44,901 --> 00:20:48,900
at 600 rounds a minute and kill a man in an instant,

275
00:20:48,901 --> 00:20:51,620
it couldn't always penetrate car bodies.

276
00:20:57,200 --> 00:21:00,220
At the time, there was only one handheld gun

277
00:21:00,221 --> 00:21:02,940
that guaranteed the power to splinter a V8-4,

278
00:21:03,500 --> 00:21:05,220
the Browning Automatic Rifle,

279
00:21:05,780 --> 00:21:07,820
otherwise known as the BAR.

280
00:21:09,300 --> 00:21:11,420
It's a very powerful firearm.

281
00:21:11,720 --> 00:21:13,680
It's more powerful than the Thompson.

282
00:21:14,380 --> 00:21:16,760
It was probably the most powerful portable firearm

283
00:21:16,761 --> 00:21:19,120
that was available to the gangsters.

284
00:21:19,620 --> 00:21:21,540
This gun here is an exact replica

285
00:21:21,541 --> 00:21:25,660
of Clyde Bauer's personal 1918 Browning Onimatic rifle.

286
00:21:25,800 --> 00:21:26,700
He named the scatter gun.

287
00:21:27,400 --> 00:21:30,300
It was captured from the gang in Dexter, Iowa.

288
00:21:31,480 --> 00:21:34,740
And basically, it's a standard 1918 BAR

289
00:21:34,741 --> 00:21:37,380
that three or four inches of the barrel has been removed,

290
00:21:37,780 --> 00:21:41,680
and the stock has been cut off just past the buffer tube.

291
00:21:42,780 --> 00:21:45,340
Clyde basically cut these guns down to make them more portable

292
00:21:45,341 --> 00:21:48,260
and makes a great gun for a motorized bandit.

293
00:21:50,720 --> 00:21:53,440
Clyde trained Bonnie in the use of firearms,

294
00:21:53,620 --> 00:21:55,640
and she became a very good marksman herself,

295
00:21:55,880 --> 00:21:58,320
not only using smaller caliber weapons,

296
00:21:58,420 --> 00:22:00,520
but she also used the BAR.

297
00:22:01,180 --> 00:22:03,860
And the BAR is a heavy automatic rifle

298
00:22:03,861 --> 00:22:05,920
that fires a .30-06 round.

299
00:22:06,880 --> 00:22:09,260
Bonnie didn't weigh much more than 100 pounds,

300
00:22:09,860 --> 00:22:15,040
and yet she wielded one of the nastiest weapons ever used,

301
00:22:15,041 --> 00:22:20,580
the BAR.

302
00:22:20,581 --> 00:22:27,620
The BAR had been introduced by the Army at the end of World War I.

303
00:22:27,621 --> 00:22:31,020
When a gangster who knew how to handle one fired a BAR,

304
00:22:31,021 --> 00:22:34,360
all hell broke loose.

305
00:22:34,361 --> 00:22:38,560
It was a rock that got a lot of gangsters out of ambush situations.

306
00:22:38,561 --> 00:22:44,700
It gave them the firepower and the ability to break out of almost insurmountable odds.

307
00:22:44,701 --> 00:22:47,240
If you just want to throw up a lot of lead to cover your escape,

308
00:22:47,380 --> 00:22:51,160
it's great because the human factor, if you're firing at someone,

309
00:22:51,900 --> 00:22:54,480
they're generally going to duck unless they've been through combat.

310
00:22:55,240 --> 00:22:58,380
And especially with something like this, it has a booming report,

311
00:22:58,560 --> 00:22:59,520
you're throwing a lot of lead out.

312
00:22:59,880 --> 00:23:01,100
It makes people duck.

313
00:23:05,980 --> 00:23:10,080
One of the most famous shootouts with the BAR involved Bonnie and Clyde.

314
00:23:10,081 --> 00:23:13,680
In 1933, they were trapped in an apartment building

315
00:23:13,780 --> 00:23:15,120
in Joplin, Missouri.

316
00:23:15,220 --> 00:23:16,980
Police had surrounded the building.

317
00:23:19,320 --> 00:23:21,460
The Barrel Gang opened up from the second floor

318
00:23:21,560 --> 00:23:25,500
of a two-story stone apartment with three BARs.

319
00:23:25,600 --> 00:23:28,500
As the guns rattled on, Bonnie yelled downstairs,

320
00:23:28,600 --> 00:23:31,400
turn it on them, Clyde, we're coming down.

321
00:23:31,500 --> 00:23:32,900
The Barrel Gang came down the stairs

322
00:23:33,000 --> 00:23:34,900
with Bonnie lugging a BAR.

323
00:23:35,000 --> 00:23:37,900
She turned on a large oak tree, behind which

324
00:23:37,901 --> 00:23:40,700
which was Sergeant G.B. Kaler of the Missouri Highway Patrol.

325
00:23:43,340 --> 00:23:45,580
The splinters forced him backward over a stump,

326
00:23:45,581 --> 00:23:49,740
and he fell down with only one bullet in his gun.

327
00:23:49,741 --> 00:23:52,380
He would later say, after they had escaped,

328
00:23:52,381 --> 00:23:55,260
that that little redheaded woman filled my face up with splinters

329
00:23:55,261 --> 00:23:56,620
with one of those damn guns.

330
00:24:01,420 --> 00:24:03,820
One gangster who lived by his BAR

331
00:24:03,821 --> 00:24:06,300
and eventually died by it was Slim Gray.

332
00:24:07,900 --> 00:24:11,700
Though Gray was not as well known as some of his more infamous colleagues,

333
00:24:11,980 --> 00:24:14,260
he had an air of invincibility about him.

334
00:24:17,620 --> 00:24:20,700
Slim was a minor league crook who was hiding out in Chicago

335
00:24:20,701 --> 00:24:23,620
when police and the FBI showed up outside his door.

336
00:24:25,520 --> 00:24:29,780
Gray fervently believed that his BAR and bulletproof vest would protect him.

337
00:24:31,020 --> 00:24:32,720
He was very wrong.

338
00:24:32,721 --> 00:24:39,200
Slim Gray, all 6 feet 2 and 125 pounds of him,

339
00:24:40,100 --> 00:24:43,780
came down the back stairs with a bulletproof vest

340
00:24:43,781 --> 00:24:45,120
and lugging a BAR.

341
00:24:46,000 --> 00:24:48,860
At the bottom of the stairs, a gunfight broke out.

342
00:24:49,880 --> 00:24:55,060
The agent with the .351 rifle shot Slim Gray

343
00:24:55,061 --> 00:24:57,660
straight through the vest, and he flopped down in the yard.

344
00:24:59,000 --> 00:25:01,120
They took him to the hospital where he died,

345
00:25:01,121 --> 00:25:03,200
and they dug out the bullet,

346
00:25:03,400 --> 00:25:05,900
which went through the bulletproof vest's front panel

347
00:25:05,901 --> 00:25:08,300
and flattened against the back panel.

348
00:25:08,940 --> 00:25:10,280
It is occasionally displayed

349
00:25:10,281 --> 00:25:11,880
by the Federal Bureau of Investigation

350
00:25:11,881 --> 00:25:14,080
as one of its grizzliest gangster souvenirs.

351
00:25:17,200 --> 00:25:19,680
Big automatic weapons were quickly becoming

352
00:25:19,681 --> 00:25:20,820
the order of the day.

353
00:25:21,540 --> 00:25:23,600
Pretty boy Floyd loved machine guns.

354
00:25:24,020 --> 00:25:26,540
So, of course, did machine gun Kelly.

355
00:25:26,541 --> 00:25:34,960
But perhaps the best shot with a machine gun during the gangster era was a lesser-known criminal named Vern Miller.

356
00:25:38,520 --> 00:25:44,080
Like many of the gangsters of the 1920s, Miller was trained by the Army during World War I.

357
00:25:47,080 --> 00:25:52,280
After the war, he returned home to South Dakota, where he became a policeman and then a sheriff.

358
00:25:52,281 --> 00:25:59,080
But when Miller was caught in Besley, he fled and began a life of crime.

359
00:26:00,520 --> 00:26:04,180
His training as a police officer made him an excellent wheel man.

360
00:26:07,000 --> 00:26:13,360
Gangsters like Alvin Karpis and Pretty Boy Floyd hired Miller for his ability to drive fast and safe.

361
00:26:16,480 --> 00:26:19,620
But it was his military background that brought him fame.

362
00:26:19,621 --> 00:26:22,620
Vern Miller was reportedly so good with a machine gun,

363
00:26:22,720 --> 00:26:25,620
any machine gun, that he could write his name in bullets.

364
00:26:30,640 --> 00:26:33,640
Miller pushed his luck when he crossed Al Capone,

365
00:26:33,740 --> 00:26:35,640
and Capone went looking for him.

366
00:26:38,500 --> 00:26:40,140
Capone sent three of his flat-nosed boys

367
00:26:40,240 --> 00:26:41,640
to a place called Fox River.

368
00:26:41,740 --> 00:26:43,140
They mixed it up with Miller.

369
00:26:43,141 --> 00:26:44,040
Miller.

370
00:26:50,320 --> 00:26:52,080
When the cops got to the scene, they

371
00:26:52,180 --> 00:26:54,760
called it the Fox River Massacre, assuming

372
00:26:54,860 --> 00:26:58,220
that these first class hitters of Capone's had been wiped out

373
00:26:58,320 --> 00:26:59,360
by an army.

374
00:26:59,460 --> 00:27:02,800
They had, in fact, been wiped out by an army of one, Vern Miller.

375
00:27:06,500 --> 00:27:08,700
But Miller's luck soon ran out.

376
00:27:08,800 --> 00:27:11,440
When he crossed Detroit's notorious Purple Gang,

377
00:27:11,441 --> 00:27:14,440
He paid the price.

378
00:27:14,540 --> 00:27:17,680
A couple of hitters from the Purple Gang in Detroit

379
00:27:17,780 --> 00:27:23,080
who died in a similar fashion to Capone's boys in Fox River.

380
00:27:23,180 --> 00:27:27,220
Sometime thereafter, someone left Vern Miller's bullet-riddled

381
00:27:27,320 --> 00:27:30,060
body as a Detroit roadside decoration.

382
00:27:30,160 --> 00:27:31,400
The Purple Gang had no comment.

383
00:27:35,000 --> 00:27:37,360
As the bodies piled up, law enforcement

384
00:27:37,460 --> 00:27:40,100
began to understand that gangsters had better guns

385
00:27:40,101 --> 00:27:42,940
and were willing to use them at any cost.

386
00:27:44,080 --> 00:27:46,380
To win the battle against crime,

387
00:27:46,680 --> 00:27:48,720
the police would have to do the same.

388
00:27:51,980 --> 00:27:54,940
The Thompson submachine gun had a few nicknames

389
00:27:54,941 --> 00:27:56,200
other than the Tommy gun.

390
00:27:56,680 --> 00:27:58,880
It was also referred to as the Chopper,

391
00:27:59,220 --> 00:28:01,220
the Gat, and the Chicago Typewriter.

392
00:28:02,320 --> 00:28:05,100
Gangster guns will continue on Modern Marvel.

393
00:28:10,100 --> 00:28:16,340
By the early 1930s, the gangsters of the big cities and the bandits of the Midwest were legends.

394
00:28:18,920 --> 00:28:24,220
The public loved their Robin Hood image and relished every story of shootouts and holdups.

395
00:28:26,880 --> 00:28:29,880
Police and federal agents had a much different view.

396
00:28:32,280 --> 00:28:38,000
The outlaws they faced were some of the best armed criminals in history and some of the most ruthless.

397
00:28:40,100 --> 00:28:44,600
One of the most deadly gangsters of the day was Lester Gillis,

398
00:28:44,601 --> 00:28:48,100
otherwise known as Babyface Nelson.

399
00:28:48,101 --> 00:28:53,100
Nelson prided himself on his use of firearms

400
00:28:53,101 --> 00:28:56,100
and his willingness to kill members of law enforcement.

401
00:28:56,101 --> 00:29:02,100
In fact, when it came to the police, Nelson never ran,

402
00:29:02,101 --> 00:29:05,100
even on the occasions when he himself was shot.

403
00:29:05,101 --> 00:29:07,100
when he himself was shot.

404
00:29:10,600 --> 00:29:12,440
He would always turn and fight,

405
00:29:12,441 --> 00:29:16,440
sometimes to the dismay of his partners in crime.

406
00:29:32,900 --> 00:29:38,060
On August 14, 1934, Nelson, his wife, and another outlaw were driving near Barrington,

407
00:29:38,061 --> 00:29:42,060
Illinois.

408
00:29:42,061 --> 00:29:46,200
When the men spotted two cars of federal agents headed in the opposite direction, the

409
00:29:46,201 --> 00:29:49,800
The chase was on, with Nelson in hot pursuit.

410
00:29:49,900 --> 00:29:53,940
A rolling gun battle ensued.

411
00:29:54,040 --> 00:29:57,380
One must understand that Nelson had a particular hatred

412
00:29:57,480 --> 00:29:58,680
for federal agents.

413
00:29:58,780 --> 00:30:01,280
He carried pictures, tag numbers, and descriptions of them

414
00:30:01,380 --> 00:30:03,020
in his car and actively hunted them.

415
00:30:06,980 --> 00:30:08,760
The cars eventually wound up ditched

416
00:30:08,860 --> 00:30:11,820
on the side of the road, but the guns were still roaring.

417
00:30:16,200 --> 00:30:25,620
And it was just a hellish firefight with hundreds of rounds being sprayed all over the area, pinning down Babyface Nelson.

418
00:30:25,980 --> 00:30:30,320
And he told his partner, he said, I've had enough of this.

419
00:30:30,720 --> 00:30:34,500
And he charged the FBI agents, guns a-blazing.

420
00:30:37,560 --> 00:30:40,420
It was Babyface Nelson's last hurrah.

421
00:30:40,421 --> 00:30:46,820
Not even his trusted Tommy gun could save him from the blaze of bullets fired by the doomed FBI agents.

422
00:30:52,660 --> 00:30:56,300
Nelson tried to drive away, but he had 17 holes in him.

423
00:30:57,120 --> 00:31:00,620
They took him to Father Philip Coughlin's house in Chicago.

424
00:31:01,640 --> 00:31:05,480
They took him to another safe house where he was given the last rites,

425
00:31:05,481 --> 00:31:08,260
and they wrapped the dying gangster in the blanket

426
00:31:08,261 --> 00:31:11,100
that he had been clothed in.

427
00:31:11,380 --> 00:31:14,060
And when he died, they laid him out on a tombstone in Nile Center.

428
00:31:15,860 --> 00:31:19,060
If Babyface Nelson was the most deadly individual

429
00:31:19,061 --> 00:31:22,600
of the gangster era, the most deadly gang by far

430
00:31:22,601 --> 00:31:25,920
was the outfit run by Clyde Barrow and Bonnie Parker.

431
00:31:28,480 --> 00:31:31,620
The Barrow gang carried the biggest, baddest guns.

432
00:31:32,280 --> 00:31:34,580
In addition to Clyde's homemade whippet guns,

433
00:31:34,581 --> 00:31:36,340
the BAR was a favorite.

434
00:31:39,720 --> 00:31:43,500
The Barrows were ruthless, especially toward law enforcement.

435
00:31:46,760 --> 00:31:50,780
Their main motivation was making money as easily as possible

436
00:31:50,781 --> 00:31:52,260
and not getting caught.

437
00:31:54,720 --> 00:31:56,900
If someone got hurt along the way,

438
00:31:57,100 --> 00:31:59,300
that was just the price of doing business.

439
00:32:04,580 --> 00:32:06,620
Like many of the criminals of the time,

440
00:32:06,720 --> 00:32:09,820
Bonnie and Clyde were seemingly unafraid of death,

441
00:32:09,920 --> 00:32:12,520
partly because of the intense firepower they carried.

442
00:32:15,320 --> 00:32:17,760
They had shot their way out of so many tight spots

443
00:32:17,860 --> 00:32:20,300
and killed so many law enforcement officers

444
00:32:20,400 --> 00:32:22,200
that they were greatly feared by the police.

445
00:32:25,860 --> 00:32:29,140
A lawman named Frank Hammer had assembled a posse of six men

446
00:32:29,240 --> 00:32:30,800
to hunt down Bonnie and Clyde.

447
00:32:30,801 --> 00:32:40,640
On the morning of May 23, 1934, outside Gibson, Louisiana, Bonnie and Clyde approached a gangster mailbox.

448
00:32:41,600 --> 00:32:45,820
Hammer and his men were waiting, with an arsenal that would have made the Barrow gang proud.

449
00:32:49,100 --> 00:32:57,960
They stood up on the hill, two BARs, a Remington 8 police rifle in .35 caliber, three Remington guns in .30.

450
00:32:58,960 --> 00:33:00,560
Clyde Barrow pulled up to the mailbox,

451
00:33:01,000 --> 00:33:03,260
took his foot off the clutch, and never heard the hammer fall.

452
00:33:11,340 --> 00:33:14,900
It is not by treachery that these men ambushed him this way.

453
00:33:15,380 --> 00:33:17,860
And it is not because Clyde was so bad.

454
00:33:18,160 --> 00:33:20,540
It was because Clyde Barrow was that good.

455
00:33:21,240 --> 00:33:22,820
And had they given him any chance,

456
00:33:23,180 --> 00:33:25,280
someone might not have gone home to his wife.

457
00:33:25,281 --> 00:33:30,280
Clyde, struck by 48 rounds, died immediately.

458
00:33:30,380 --> 00:33:32,280
Bonnie was hit 37 times.

459
00:33:35,820 --> 00:33:38,220
Police cautiously approached the car.

460
00:33:38,320 --> 00:33:40,220
When they opened the passenger door,

461
00:33:40,320 --> 00:33:43,140
Bonnie fell into the arms of Officer Ted Henton.

462
00:33:46,300 --> 00:33:50,080
She had a slight pulse, but by the time Henton tumbled her

463
00:33:50,180 --> 00:33:52,280
back into the car, she was dead.

464
00:33:55,280 --> 00:34:05,240
The death of Bonnie and Clyde was the beginning of the end of the gangster era.

465
00:34:06,980 --> 00:34:11,500
Many of the most famous gangsters and outlaws of the time would die in similar ways.

466
00:34:14,480 --> 00:34:19,720
The powerful guns they had helped make famous were now turned against them by the forces of law and order.

467
00:34:19,721 --> 00:34:28,480
In the world of gangster guns, the philosophy of an eye for an eye had become a bitter reality.

468
00:34:35,060 --> 00:34:41,060
Gangster Machine Gun Kelly reputedly coined the nickname G-Men while in the custody of law enforcement agents.

469
00:34:41,820 --> 00:34:48,300
Short for Government Man, the heroic G-Men quickly became synonymous with the FBI in the public's imagination.

470
00:34:48,301 --> 00:34:53,300
Gangster Guns will continue on Modern Marvels.

471
00:34:55,300 --> 00:35:00,260
By the mid-1930s, the age of gangsters and their guns was on the way out.

472
00:35:02,800 --> 00:35:10,120
In 1933, the Volstead Act was repealed and legalized liquor again pumped through the veins of the United States.

473
00:35:10,121 --> 00:35:19,380
That meant now there was not great gobs of money to be made in illicit trafficking of alcohol.

474
00:35:20,080 --> 00:35:23,100
And this was really the beginning of the end for the great gangs.

475
00:35:23,380 --> 00:35:29,120
This was their money cow, their cash cow, that drove the operation.

476
00:35:30,060 --> 00:35:31,100
That was ended.

477
00:35:34,180 --> 00:35:39,240
In the big cities, reform-minded mayors and city councils were voted into office.

478
00:35:40,120 --> 00:35:47,240
Without the large amount of cash the gangs used to have to throw around, it became nearly

479
00:35:47,241 --> 00:35:49,220
impossible to bribe public officials.

480
00:35:53,300 --> 00:35:59,140
The 1934 National Firearms Act was an attempt by lawmakers to make it tougher for criminals

481
00:35:59,141 --> 00:36:00,300
to access guns.

482
00:36:04,620 --> 00:36:09,840
During the early days of the reign of terror, gangsters had been careful not to kill civilians.

483
00:36:10,120 --> 00:36:15,120
But as their guns became more powerful and their desperation more severe,

484
00:36:15,121 --> 00:36:18,120
errant shots began hitting the wrong people.

485
00:36:20,120 --> 00:36:26,120
An increasingly angry public grew weary of innocent bystanders, especially children,

486
00:36:26,121 --> 00:36:28,120
falling victim to the guns of hoodlums.

487
00:36:33,120 --> 00:36:37,120
In addition, police communications had greatly improved.

488
00:36:37,121 --> 00:36:39,120
No longer was an outlaw free and clear

489
00:36:39,121 --> 00:36:41,120
if he could lose a chasing police car.

490
00:36:46,120 --> 00:36:50,120
Now, every police car was equipped with two-way radios.

491
00:36:50,121 --> 00:36:53,120
Catching escaping bandits became much easier.

492
00:36:59,120 --> 00:37:03,120
On top of it all, the times had, quite simply, changed.

493
00:37:03,121 --> 00:37:05,120
The rumblings of war coming from Europe

494
00:37:05,121 --> 00:37:10,560
coming from Europe swallowed the innocence of the previous age, especially in small-town America.

495
00:37:11,920 --> 00:37:17,101
The dislocation of the rural folk had occurred by then.

496
00:37:17,102 --> 00:37:20,680
They had moved to the cities. They had

497
00:37:20,681 --> 00:37:28,751
moved to California by the hundreds of thousands. That era was over.

498
00:37:28,752 --> 00:37:31,600
Society had changed, and

499
00:37:31,601 --> 00:37:37,680
no longer did you have the right social milieu for social banditry,

500
00:37:37,880 --> 00:37:39,740
kind of the Robin Hoods of the Southwest.

501
00:37:45,180 --> 00:37:48,360
A less tolerant public, stronger law enforcement,

502
00:37:48,800 --> 00:37:51,740
and better tactics and equipment with which to catch the crooks

503
00:37:51,741 --> 00:37:54,460
made the life of a gangster all but impossible.

504
00:37:58,120 --> 00:38:00,600
Even their arsenals of powerful guns,

505
00:38:00,601 --> 00:38:03,840
which they had relied on for nearly 20 years to do their bidding,

506
00:38:04,220 --> 00:38:06,600
were ineffective against the tides of change.

507
00:38:09,640 --> 00:38:13,040
Perhaps the event that best defined the end of the gangster age

508
00:38:13,041 --> 00:38:15,100
was the death of pretty boy Floyd.

509
00:38:17,760 --> 00:38:21,700
In November 1934, a woman named Ellen Conkle

510
00:38:21,701 --> 00:38:24,340
heard a knock at the door of her Ohio farmhouse.

511
00:38:25,840 --> 00:38:28,960
It was morning, and a handsome stranger was there

512
00:38:28,961 --> 00:38:30,400
asking for something to eat.

513
00:38:30,600 --> 00:38:33,100
Conkle had given food to strangers before,

514
00:38:33,101 --> 00:38:35,100
so she invited the men inside.

515
00:38:39,100 --> 00:38:42,100
For the next half hour, he ate without saying much.

516
00:38:42,101 --> 00:38:45,600
When he finished, he declared the meal was fit for a king.

517
00:38:47,600 --> 00:38:51,100
The man gave Ellen Conkle a dollar for the breakfast,

518
00:38:51,101 --> 00:38:53,100
about four times what it was worth.

519
00:38:55,600 --> 00:38:59,100
A moment later, Conkle's yard filled up with police cars,

520
00:38:59,101 --> 00:39:03,340
cars, and the stranger took off running across her cornfield.

521
00:39:03,440 --> 00:39:05,940
They saw him emerging from this farmhouse,

522
00:39:06,040 --> 00:39:08,640
and he made a dash across a field.

523
00:39:13,340 --> 00:39:16,660
And they simply executed him.

524
00:39:16,760 --> 00:39:20,760
A half dozen rifles opened up on him.

525
00:39:24,060 --> 00:39:29,100
And in fact, when they came upon him in this field,

526
00:39:29,101 --> 00:39:33,140
there's some fairly strong evidence that he was still alive,

527
00:39:33,360 --> 00:39:35,720
and they shot him there at point-blank range.

528
00:39:36,800 --> 00:39:39,320
They weren't concerned with courts in those days,

529
00:39:39,480 --> 00:39:42,100
and when they found somebody

530
00:39:42,101 --> 00:39:45,520
and that guy raised a finger to resist or a gun,

531
00:39:46,820 --> 00:39:49,620
they just shot him, and it was the end of it.

532
00:39:53,620 --> 00:39:55,600
The police told Ellen Conkle

533
00:39:55,601 --> 00:39:57,800
that the fellow she had just given breakfast to

534
00:39:57,801 --> 00:39:59,940
was none other than Pretty Boy Floyd.

535
00:40:02,880 --> 00:40:06,040
The man who had hunted down Floyd was Melvin Purvis,

536
00:40:06,360 --> 00:40:08,480
the head of the FBI's Chicago office.

537
00:40:11,340 --> 00:40:13,560
The story of gangsters and their guns

538
00:40:13,561 --> 00:40:16,500
can be summed up in the moment Purvis stood next to the body

539
00:40:16,501 --> 00:40:18,940
of Pretty Boy Floyd in that Ohio field.

540
00:40:21,340 --> 00:40:24,520
Pretty Boy Floyd, on his person, has two .45 automatics.

541
00:40:24,600 --> 00:40:25,760
One is made in 1918.

542
00:40:26,100 --> 00:40:27,540
One is about a year and a half younger.

543
00:40:27,800 --> 00:40:31,680
Melvin Purvis, on his person, has a 1925

544
00:40:31,780 --> 00:40:34,600
Colt commercial automatic, nickel plated.

545
00:40:34,700 --> 00:40:37,880
And at the end of the day, what you must know is both men

546
00:40:37,980 --> 00:40:40,880
chose not similar guns, but exactly the same gun.

547
00:40:40,980 --> 00:40:43,080
And they chose them for the same reason.

548
00:40:43,180 --> 00:40:46,180
And what separated these two men was not their guns,

549
00:40:46,280 --> 00:40:48,420
but their choice in life and their character.

550
00:40:51,520 --> 00:40:53,520
Since the end of the gangster era,

551
00:40:53,620 --> 00:40:56,000
there have been few individuals in the modern age

552
00:40:56,001 --> 00:40:58,500
who have used guns like the outlaws of old.

553
00:41:00,940 --> 00:41:03,840
Experts believe that the nature of crime has changed.

554
00:41:06,300 --> 00:41:08,480
Where gangsters of old fought battles

555
00:41:08,481 --> 00:41:11,060
against large institutions like police and banks,

556
00:41:11,600 --> 00:41:14,180
today's criminals are less willing to take on the world.

557
00:41:16,180 --> 00:41:18,740
Instead, crimes against people and property

558
00:41:18,741 --> 00:41:22,260
are more common than during the daring days of the gangster age.

559
00:41:26,000 --> 00:41:29,300
Another reason the age died in the days before World War II

560
00:41:29,301 --> 00:41:33,260
was the spirit that made the criminals stand out was extinguished.

561
00:41:35,800 --> 00:41:39,880
Few outlaws of the modern age exhibit the kind of willingness to die

562
00:41:39,881 --> 00:41:44,380
that many of the gangsters of the 1920s and 30s lived with every day.

563
00:41:47,040 --> 00:41:49,380
Because their exploits were so outrageous

564
00:41:49,381 --> 00:41:52,880
and because of their willingness to use the best guns of their time,

565
00:41:53,280 --> 00:41:54,860
they continue to fascinate.

566
00:41:56,000 --> 00:42:03,000
Today, weapons used by the gangsters and motorized bandits are highly prized by collectors.

567
00:42:03,001 --> 00:42:08,000
Transferable Browning Onamac rifles for the general public, they've almost doubled in

568
00:42:08,001 --> 00:42:10,000
price in the last year.

569
00:42:10,001 --> 00:42:15,000
Even what we call dealer samples that are available to dealers only, class three dealers,

570
00:42:15,001 --> 00:42:19,000
have gone up to the realm of $3,500.

571
00:42:19,001 --> 00:42:25,000
It's a testament to the stained power of the image gangsters portray.

572
00:42:25,001 --> 00:42:29,100
They used the state-of-the-art weaponry.

573
00:42:29,860 --> 00:42:43,860
So here they were fighting lawmen with BARs, Thompson submachine guns, high-powered hunting rifles, handguns of all sorts, plus a number of weapons that they modified

574
00:42:46,704 --> 00:42:47,820
themselves.

575
00:42:47,821 --> 00:42:53,020
They didn't attack, for the most part, the average citizen.

576
00:42:53,021 --> 00:43:02,320
Most of their great gun battles were with lawmen, and many times they came out on the winning side of that gun battle.

577
00:43:03,380 --> 00:43:09,040
They had magnificent escapes with all odds stacked against them.

578
00:43:09,041 --> 00:43:21,119
And so I think Americans in general tend to romanticize figures such as that who take on the establishment with nothing but their own guile, their own courage, their

579
00:43:21,120 --> 00:43:24,340
own nerve, their own savvy and intelligence.

580
00:43:26,500 --> 00:43:31,060
But in the end, the outlaws of the 20s and 30s were beaten at their own game.

581
00:43:35,620 --> 00:43:36,920
Police got smarter.

582
00:43:36,921 --> 00:43:38,700
The public became intolerant.

583
00:43:39,040 --> 00:43:45,500
and the guns on which the criminals relied were just as powerful and just as effective in the hands of an FBI agent.

584
00:43:52,220 --> 00:43:57,680
Within a few short years, the bloody legacy of gangsters and their guns was brought to an end.

585
00:43:58,720 --> 00:44:03,400
Those who chose to live by their guns had died by them just as violently.

586
00:44:03,401 --> 00:44:07,300
It was an era unique in American history,

587
00:44:07,840 --> 00:44:10,140
one that most likely will never happen again.

588
00:44:11,180 --> 00:44:13,600
It is a time that will be forever defined

589
00:44:13,601 --> 00:44:16,920
by raging gun battles between the forces of good and evil,

590
00:44:17,980 --> 00:44:20,100
a time when the nation stood still

591
00:44:20,101 --> 00:44:24,500
and was mesmerized by the sights and sounds of gangster guns.

